---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { SITE, DOORS, PRACTICE, PUBLICATIONS } from '../consts';

const pub = PUBLICATIONS[0];

const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Organization',
  name: SITE.name,
  url: SITE.origin,
  description: SITE.description,
  slogan: SITE.hero,
  founder: {
    '@type': 'Person',
    name: SITE.founder,
    url: SITE.founderSite,
  },
  sameAs: [SITE.linkedin, SITE.founderSite],
};
---

<Base
  title="Imagination Applied"
  description={SITE.description}
  path="/"
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <section class="wrap hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">{SITE.tagline}</p>
    <h1>{SITE.hero}</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      AI can make almost anything. The real leadership challenge is deciding what is
      worth making—and creating the conditions to make it real.
    </p>
    <div class="actions">
      <a class="btn" href="/contact/">Start a conversation</a>
      <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="/how-we-work/">See how we work</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap section-rule">
    <h2>The problem is not capability. It is direction.</h2>
    <p>
      Organizations are surrounded by possibility and starved for direction. They can
      generate more options, more content, more analysis than ever. But more output does
      not produce shared meaning, good judgment, or movement.
    </p>
    <p>
      Teams can be competent and still be in different plays. Leaders can move quickly and
      still move in the wrong direction. AI can produce a beautiful answer to a question
      nobody meant to ask.
    </p>
    <p>
      That decision—what is worth making—is not hidden inside a better prompt. It lives in
      interpretation. In the point of view you bring, the people and tools you put in the
      room, and what you learn when the idea finally has to move.
    </p>
    <p class="pull">Artistic Intelligence is what we use when the answer is not waiting to be found—it has to be made.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap section-rule">
    <h2>Three ways to work together</h2>
    <p class="muted">
      Every engagement starts with the same question: what is this work actually about?
    </p>
    <ul class="cards">
      {
        DOORS.map((door) => (
          <li class="card card-link">
            <h3><a href={door.href}>{door.title}</a></h3>
            <p>{door.for}</p>
            <p class="outcome">
              <strong>Outcome</strong>
              {door.outcome}
            </p>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap section-rule">
    <h2>The method: direct, cast, rehearse</h2>
    <p>
      Our practice is called TheaterThink. It is not a metaphor we decorate the work with.
      It is how the work is actually run: three moves, in order.
    </p>
    <ul class="cards">
      {
        PRACTICE.map((step) => (
          <li class="card">
            <h3>{step.name}</h3>
            <p class="muted">{step.subtitle}</p>
            <p>{step.oneLiner}</p>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>
    <p style="margin-top:1.5rem">
      <a href="/how-we-work/">How we work, in detail →</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap section-rule">
    <h2>We publish what we find</h2>
    <p>
      Advice about AI adoption is cheap. We test ours, publish the code and the
      checksums, and state the limitations in the body of the work rather than in a
      footnote.
    </p>
    <div class="card" style="margin-top:1.5rem;max-width:38rem">
      <p class="eyebrow">{pub.series}</p>
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">
        <a href={`/publications/${pub.slug}/`}>{pub.title}</a>
      </h3>
      <p>{pub.description}</p>
      <p class="outcome">
        <strong>Read</strong>
        Full analysis, figures, reproducible code, and the strongest counterargument.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap section-rule">
    <h2>Who is behind this</h2>
    <p>
      Imagination Applied was founded by <a href={SITE.founderSite}>Josh Penzell</a>—keynote
      speaker, advisor, and theater director by training. He is the forthcoming author of
      <em>Artistic Intelligence</em>, and the public voice of this work.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you are here to book Josh for a stage, that lives on
      <a href={SITE.founderSite}>joshpenzell.com</a>. If you are here to change how your
      organization works, you are in the right place.
    </p>
    <div class="actions">
      <a class="btn" href="/contact/">Start a conversation</a>
      <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="/about/">About the company</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
